{"input": "What did the group discuss about the trend of teacher recruiting and the reasons for it, and steps they had taken?", "answers": ["Although there were no exact figures,they had seen over recent years that they were only recruiting to about 65 percent of targets. It was partially due to the financial incentives, but that was not all of the problems. Because this issue was not only in Wales, but a common issue in all over the world. For example, in England, despite higher financial incentives to join ITE courses, people still were not getting onto those courses. To solve this problem, they had taken several steps including making sure their ITE provision was world class, embarking on the first national ITE recruitment marketing exercise, etc. In this process, Llyr Gruffydd had a doubt that the level of ambition did not reflect the level of action that was needed actually. Kirsty Williams responded that different statistics told different stories. They obviously understood there were many steps they had to take, but they were trying instead of just sitting back and waiting."], "length": 13883, "dataset": "qmsum", "pred": "The group discussed the trend of teacher recruiting and the reasons for it, and steps they had taken. They mentioned that there was still a job of work to understand and respond to the needs, and that they had taken steps such as making sure that their ITE provision was world class, offering financial incentives, and embarking on an IT recruitment marketing exercise."}
